Error.isError performs an internal brand check for native error objects. 

The specification checks the [ [ErrorData]] internal slot. [3] The method returns true for Error, native error subclasses, and AggregateError instances, including instances created in another realm. It returns false for primitives and objects that only imitate an error's properties or string tag. The proposal explains why instanceof and Object.prototype.toString are insufficient brand checks.  ... // instanceof compares against one realm's Error constructor.
const crossRealm = vm.runInNewContext("new TypeError('boom')");
console.log(crossRealm instanceof Error);
// false
// A string tag makes Object.prototype.toString report a plain object as an error.
const imitation = { name: "TypeError", message: "boom", [Symbol.toStringTag]: "Error" };
console.log(Object.prototype.toString.call(imitation));
// [object Error]
// The same tag hides a native error from that check.
 ... import vm from "node:vm";
const crossRealm = vm.runInNewContext("new TypeError('boom')");
console.log(Error.isError(crossRealm));
// true
const imitation = { name: "TypeError", message: "boom", [Symbol.toStringTag]: "Error" };
console.log(Error.isError(imitation));
// false
const tagged = new TypeError("boom");
tagged[Symbol.toStringTag] = "Object";
console.log(Error.isError(tagged));
// true
console.log(Error.isError(new AggregateError([], "boom")), Error.isError(Error.prototype), Error.isError("boom"));
// true false false

JavaScript permits any value to be thrown, so caught values still require separate handling when Error.isError returns false.  ... `Error.isError` performs an internal brand check for native error objects.

