The Iterator.concat () static method creates a new Iterator object from a list of iterable objects. The new iterator yields the values from each of the input iterables in sequence. ... Iterator.concat(it1, it2)
Iterator.concat(it1, it2, /* …, */ itN) ... The Iterator.concat () method is conceptually similar to Array 's concat () method, but it operates on any kind of iterable, and returns an iterator instead of an array. This means that the iterables can be lazily iterated, avoiding unnecessary allocation or computation. It also means that, technically, you can concatenate infinite iterables, but results from iterables after the first infinite iterable will never be reached. ...   }


Like yield*, Iterator.concat () doesn't support arguments that are not iterable (i.e., don't have the [Symbol.iterator] () method). This is because Iterator.concat () always takes ownership over its iterators and closes open iterators when the method exits. With iterable arguments, Iterator.concat () acquires iterators one-by-one and just closes the current iterator when the iteration is stopped. With iterator arguments, it's not clear whether the caller or Iterator.concat () should be responsible for closing the iterators, especially the ones that Iterator.concat () hasn't reached, so the method simply disallows non-iterable arguments. ... const map = new Map(Iterator.concat(map1, map2, map3));
 ... const it = Iterator.concat(it1(), it2(), it3());
