Content Mappers are TypeScript's plugin surface for checking file types the compiler cannot parse itself — the TypeScript 7.1 API roadmap identifies them as the TS Server plugin replacement needed by Vue. 

Vize ships a conforming content mapper inside the vize npm package: a tsgo build with content-mapper support spawns vize content-mapper and checks .vue files directly — hover, go-to-definition, rename, completions, and diagnostics all map back to your authored SFC, with no parallel .vue.ts project to materialize. ... Content mappers run external programs. TypeScript 7.1 and later accept them with tsc --runExternalCode. tsrs accepts the same flag and the same contentMappers entry. The conformance revision below qualifies one host build.  ...     "module": "preserve",
    "strict": true
  },
  "contentMappers": [
    {
      "package": "vize",
      "extensions": [".vue"]
    }
  ],
  "include": ["src"]
 ... In VS Code, when a trusted workspace uses a content-mapper-enabled TypeScript host, the Vize extension registers .vue support automatically. The same mapper then powers the editor. Turn on js/ts.contentMappers.enabled when the editor asks for it. ...   "contentMappers": [
    {
      "package": "vize",
      "extensions": [".vue"],
      "options": { "optionsApi": false }
    }
 ... The conformance workflow builds the native compiler from microsoft/TypeScript at exact revision d6c4afddb2c55f4a9dea7b59293a99a8fdea1799, the merged Content Mappers round 2 change. It runs the full CLI, build-mode and LSP suites through packed npm artifacts, including authored Vue declaration-map emission and navigation from a separately packed consumer back to .vue source.  ... - TypeScript 7.1 or newer is the recommended host. tsrs can run the same mapper config. Confirm that your installed compiler and editor expose content mappers before you rely on them in CI. vize check remains available for the packaged checker.

TypeScript 7.1 beta ships an official plugin point called content mappers, and Johnson Chu released Vue Language Tools v4 alpha on top of it. TypeScript’s own tsc now type-checks .vue files. No more vue-tsc, at least on the CLI. It’s an alpha, and the PR behind it is still open. ... You register a mapper for .vue in tsconfig.json.
When a .vue file enters the program, tsc asks the mapper for the virtual TypeScript and the map.
tsc type-checks the result and reports errors at the original positions. ... In v3, three packages existed only to get Vue into TypeScript from the outside: vue-tsc, @vue/typescript-plugin and @volar/typescript. In v4 all three are gone. tsc and the TS server start @vue/content-mapper themselves, because tsconfig.json tells them to. @vue/language-core, the part that turns a .vue file into TypeScript, stays. ... pnpm remove vue-tsc
pnpm add -D typescript@7.1.0-beta @vue/content-mapper@4.0.0-alpha.0

TypeScript 7.0 is not enough. Content mappers start with 7.1. ...   "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "contentMappers": [
    {
      "package": "@vue/content-mapper",
      "extensions": [".vue"],
      "options": { "target": 3.5 }
    }
  ],
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.vue"]


contentMappers sits next to compilerOptions, not inside it. Everything you had in vueCompilerOptions moves into options. Watch out: v4 ignores a leftover top-level vueCompilerOptions block without any warning.

If you use baseUrl, drop it. TypeScript 7 removed it, so write your paths relative to the config instead ("@/*": ["./src/*"]). ... tsconfig.json(13,3): error TS18068: Content mappers require the '--runExternalCode' command line flag to be enabled. ... TypeScript 7.1 beta can type-check .vue files with plain tsc through content mappers. How the migration works, and what happened when I ran it on the Directus admin app: 4.6× faster, and a lot of new errors.

